summary:
  The budget stops the ladder but leaves the standing order up, so the next
  flicker of the network puts a station that died hours ago back on.

history:
  Caught while the budget itself was being built, 2026-09-18, and it is the
  more dangerous half of the two. Stopping the knocking is visible in a log;
  an order left standing is invisible until the day it fires. netResumeTimer
  resumes on _wantsPlaying alone — deliberately, because the connection
  coming back is the one recovery people want kept — so an order that
  outlives its ladder is armed indefinitely. A station that died at three in
  the morning then starts playing at seven, which is issue #13's complaint
  word for word, and it would ship under a settings text that has just
  promised the station is left alone. The hole predates the budget: the
  2026.37 switch had it too, with the knocking off and the order still up.

target:
  package/contents/ui/main.qml

find:
              // here: _mayKnock hands a wake-up through ahead of both refusals.
              root._wantsPlaying = false;

replace:
              // here: _mayKnock hands a wake-up through ahead of both refusals.
              root._wantsPlaying = true;

expect:
  Killed by tests/test_invariants.py::test_a_spent_budget_takes_the_standing_order_down_with_it.
  Line-neutral.
